# D-135 addendum — CI-blocking flaky assertion in tests/test_r177_per_service.py::test_real_baseline_health (pre-existing on main, NOT caused by SEC-10)
# 2026-11-07; branch m9/sec10-storage-readiness (PR #332), second commit on 3e6b0899; shared 2-CPU sandbox

## the failure (CI, PR #332 head 3e6b0899, AGOS Kernel CI/CD run 42379128155)
attempt 1, suite "live test suite" (Python 3.11):  assert abs(4.106 - 4.0529359) < 0.01  -> 0.0531  FAILED (4207 passed)
attempt 2, suites 3.11 and 3.12 green (4208 passed each); suite "3.12 AGOS_AGENT_IMPL=specialists": assert abs(5.273 - 5.1705681) < 0.01 -> 0.1024 FAILED (4207 passed)
=> the volatile CI /health baseline is unaffected by SEC-10 (no storage configured => no probe); the assertion is the defect.

## cause
raw_quantiles.p95_ms = nearest-rank (scripts/r177_stop_limits.nearest_rank, the method every report declares);
k6 summary p(95) = linear interpolation at position 0.95*(n-1) (k6 TrendSink). Two different definitions: they agree within 0.01ms only
when the two neighbouring samples are within ~0.01ms of each other — luck, not a contract. Rerunning until green would be brute force.

## fix (no threshold relaxed, reports unchanged)
- scripts/r177_stop_limits.py: k6_trend_quantile(sorted_vals, q) — k6's formula.
- scripts/r177_per_service.py: raw_quantiles.p95_ms_k6_method computed from the same raw samples.
- the test compares like with like: |p95_ms_k6_method - k6 duration_p95_ms| < 0.01ms (the original intent: "the runner does not invent numbers").

